<book id="kwin-minimize2tray" lang="&language;">
<bookinfo><title>The Minimize2Tray Handbook</title>
<date>2026-11-14</date><releaseinfo>2.3</releaseinfo>
<abstract><para>Window rules and tray controls for the PhobosK fork of KWin Minimize2Tray.</para></abstract>
<keywordset><keyword>KDE</keyword><keyword>KWin</keyword><keyword>Minimize2Tray</keyword></keywordset>
</bookinfo>
<chapter id="introduction"><title>Getting started</title>
<para>Enable Minimize2Tray on the KWin Scripts page in System Settings. It requires its native tray plugin and configuration module. Both native Wayland and X11 windows are supported; some applications expose different identifying properties on each session type. Use Detect and Preview in the session where you will use the rule.</para>
<para>Press <keycombo action="simul">&Meta;&Alt;<keycap>Page Down</keycap></keycombo> to pin and hide the active main window. A tray icon belongs to one window, not every window of the application. Left-click its icon to show or hide that window on its assigned activity and virtual desktop. Windows hidden in the tray are normally removed from the taskbar, switcher and pager; attention requests may expose them on the taskbar.</para>
<para>Right-click a tray icon for Show/Hide, Unpin, Add tray rule, Configure, About and Quit. Unpin shows the window and removes its icon. Quit closes the associated window normally; other application windows may remain. About displays the version, upstream authorship and fork links. Configure opens settings for all applications.</para>
<para>Show prevents startup hiding from running again for that window. Later minimize or optional close actions can still hide it. Unpin suppresses automatic actions until no enabled rule matches or you manually pin the window again. Disabling the script restores windows hidden by it and removes its tray items.</para>
<para>In System Settings, Shortcuts, search for Minimize2Tray's actions. Minimize to tray has the default shortcut above. Add tray rule and Close or hide window to tray have no default shortcuts. Shortcut assignments are managed by KDE and are not included in configuration backups.</para>
</chapter>
<chapter id="rules"><title>Window rules</title>
<sect1 id="creating-rules"><title>Creating and reviewing rules</title>
<para>Add rule opens a manual editor. Add tray rule, from any managed tray icon or its assigned shortcut, uses KWin's native crosshair picker. Escape cancels. A new automatic rule uses the selected window's available application identity, class, instance, role and type. When needed, its exact title narrows the selection. Indistinguishable windows are rejected rather than guessed.</para>
<para>A generated rule is saved immediately, hides the selected window now, starts matching windows in the tray, and hides them there when minimized. Afterwards choose Review rule to edit it or Keep rule to finish without another dialog. If an existing rule matches, choose Edit existing rule, Create a narrower rule or Cancel. A narrower rule does not disable the wider rule: their actions still combine. Titles can change, and applications that expose identical properties cannot always be distinguished.</para>
<para>Existing values are never replaced by suggestions. Colored suggested fields are inactive until you select Use. Automatically created labels identify rules kept exactly as generated, including rules reviewed without changes. Saving a change removes the label; reverting edits before saving preserves it.</para>
<para>Rule names and descriptions organize your rules; they do not match window titles. Enable this rule controls whether its conditions and actions apply. Search filters the visible list only. Sort by orders rules by name or creation time without changing their execution order. Old rules may have unknown creation dates. Delete and Undo delete affect the pending list until Apply or OK saves it.</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="matching"><title>Matching properties</title>
<para>All selected Use conditions must match together. Separate enabled rules combine their actions. Detect window properties reads a window using KWin's picker, then lets you choose values to copy. The delay gives you time to open the desired window. Related configuration windows are hidden during picking and restored afterwards; with the script disabled only this configuration process can be hidden. Protected configuration and system windows remain ineligible as targets.</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Application ID</term><listitem><para>The launcher ID, optionally ending in .desktop. When a window omits a usable launcher ID, unique installed desktop metadata can associate its class or executable name with a launcher. Launching from systemd does not require changing the rule's class to the desktop filename. Ambiguous associations are left unresolved. Use the class obtained by Detect or choose an explicit icon instead of inventing an ID.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Window class and instance</term><listitem><para>Application-reported identifiers. Whole window class matches the instance, one space, then the class, as in KWin rules. Use Detect for the actual values; an instance is not necessarily unique to one window. Imported Class or instance conditions match either identifier.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Window role and title</term><listitem><para>A role may distinguish an editor from the main window. Titles often change with files or pages. Prefer stable properties and inspect the preview before applying.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Match mode</term><listitem><para>Exact match compares the complete value; Substring match finds a literal part; Regular expression uses a case-sensitive PCRE2 expression. Regular expressions are searched within the value; use ^ and $ to require the complete value. All modes are case-sensitive. Invalid expressions cannot be saved. Execution is bounded and values longer than 4096 characters are truncated for expression matching; a pattern that exceeds its execution limit does not match. Avoid broad patterns that target unrelated applications.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Window type and child protection</term><listitem><para>Choose normal windows, utility windows, non-modal dialogs or all supported types. Keep child windows and dialogs visible excludes children even when their type is selected. Modal dialogs, system surfaces, Minimize2Tray, KWin and configuration hosts always remain protected.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>Preview with open windows uses your unsaved edits. Click its column headings to sort by Window or Result; the default is Window, ascending. Refresh obtains current window properties. The script must be enabled to verify eligibility in this preview. A question-mark button beside each editor option shows the same explanation as its tooltip, including disabled options.</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="actions"><title>Rule actions</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Startup</term><listitem><para>Use global startup follows the Tray &amp; badges page. No startup action leaves the window alone. Pin without initially hiding adds an icon while preserving visibility. Start in tray creates an icon and hides the window when it first matches, including existing windows when rules are applied. Existing rules keep their previous explicit choices when upgraded.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Retry early startup restoration once</term><listitem><para>If an application immediately restores itself, retry hiding once within 1.5 seconds. Activation, Show and Unpin cancel the retry. A restore without activation can also be retried, so leave this off if it interferes. It only takes effect when the effective startup action is Start in tray.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Minimize to tray</term><listitem><para>On hides minimized matches in the tray. Off preserves normal minimization. Use global follows Hide on minimize. This works independently of the startup choice.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Close to tray through the optional shortcut</term><listitem><para>Assign Close or hide window to tray separately in KDE Shortcuts. It hides matching windows when enabled; otherwise that shortcut closes the window normally. It does not intercept titlebar Close, Alt+F4 or application Quit.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Quit on logout</term><listitem><para>An explicit opt-in to send SIGTERM to a verified native Wayland application's process during logout, restart or shutdown if KDE has disabled closing its matching window. It may terminate the entire process without save prompts. Requires Linux 6.13 or later and an adapter built for the running KWin version; rebuild after KWin updates. XWayland, X11, unverifiable and protected processes are left alone. Cancelling logout cannot recover a terminated application. An unavailable build lets you turn an existing opt-in off.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Override the tray icon</term><listitem><para>Off by default. Choose a local desktop file or any image format supported by Qt. A desktop file supplies only its Icon value; its commands are never executed. An enabled override replaces all automatic icon detection and affects presentation only, not matching or badges. Keep the file available; a missing icon produces a generic fallback. The first enabled override in stored rule order wins when rules overlap. Sorting the visible list does not change that order.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>Across overlapping rules, Start in tray wins over pin-only. Minimize On wins over Off, then the global default. Any matching rule can enable Close to tray, startup retry or the explicit logout action. Removing the last automatic action restores state owned by that action; manually pinned windows remain independent.</para>
<para>OK in the editor updates the pending rule list; Cancel discards editor changes. Apply or OK on the main page saves and updates running windows. Do not expect editor OK alone to save. Reload settings discards pending edits when another configuration window has saved.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>
<chapter id="tray"><title>Tray and badges</title>
<para>Global Startup applies only to rules choosing Use global startup. Its initial value is Start in tray. It never automatically targets applications without a matching rule. Existing rules retain their explicit startup behavior. Global Hide on minimize is the default for inheriting rules and manually pinned windows without matching rules. Per-rule On and Off override it.</para>
<para>Icons normally come from the application's installed desktop file, discovered by its reported ID or an unambiguous class/executable association. Otherwise the window icon is retained. User desktop-file overrides in the standard applications directories are honored. A per-rule icon override has priority over all automatic sources.</para>
<para>Applications may publish counters, progress and urgency through Unity LauncherAPI. Minimize2Tray displays the data they publish; notifications do not create unread counts. Counter as dot replaces a positive number with a dot. Counter display limit caps the displayed number, for example 99+, without changing the application's count. Progress and attention can overlay the icon. Availability depends on the application and tray host.</para>
</chapter>
<chapter id="advanced"><title>Backups and troubleshooting</title>
<para>Enable debug logging writes diagnostic messages to KWin's logs. Leave it off during normal use. If a window is not hidden, use Detect and the preview to check actual properties, AND conditions, type and child protection. On Wayland, do not use X11-only tools as evidence for a native window. For a generic icon, check the installed launcher or select a per-rule override; do not add an unrelated Application ID solely to choose an icon.</para>
<para>View as text displays read-only JSON including pending and disabled rules. Export configuration saves all rules, options and sorting to a JSON backup. Import configuration lets you select rules, options or both, merge rules or replace them, and review before Apply or OK. Adding skips identical copies and repairs conflicting rule IDs. Unselected items remain unchanged. Imported file paths must be available locally to supply icon overrides. Shortcuts and unrelated KWin settings are not included.</para>
<para>Older rule formats are migrated while preserving explicit behavior, with a backup made when saved. A creation date that was not recorded remains unknown. Invalid changes are reported; runtime reload keeps the last valid configuration. Simultaneous saves are guarded: reload if another window has changed settings rather than overwriting it.</para>
<para>Disabling the script restores managed windows and removes its tray items. KWin or Plasma restarts can temporarily interrupt tray availability. After updating native code, a new KWin session may be needed to load the new plugin; live rule reload does not replace a library already loaded in KWin. Build and installation instructions, distribution packages and project issue links are in the repository README.</para>
</chapter>
</book>
